[tool] # NeuralShell Desktop Patch v2 – Change Log This change log summarises the enhancements delivered as part of the Phase 601–700 desktop patch. Each feature has been modularised under `src/modules/` and integrated via `bindings.js` without breaking existing functionality. ## Ticket T‑01 – Vault AES‑256 (Revised) ### Added / Revised - Implemented robust AES‑256‑GCM encryption for the vault using Node’s built‑in `crypto` module. A new `vaultAPI` is exposed via `preload.js` with `encrypt()` and `decrypt()` methods that derive a 256‑bit key from the user’s passphrase using PBKDF2 (100 k iterations, SHA‑256). Each encryption uses a random 16‑byte salt and a 12‑byte IV and produces an authentication tag. The resulting cipher string is formatted as `salt:iv:cipher:tag` with each component base64‑encoded. Updated `src/modules/vaultEncryption.js` to detect the presence of `vaultAPI` and call its methods for encryption and decryption. If unavailable (e.g. when running in a browser without Node integration), the module falls back to the Web Crypto AES‑GCM implementation. This maintains compatibility with prior saved vaults while enabling stronger encryption when possible. - Saving a vault now serialises the current state and console log, prompts the user for a passphrase, encrypts the payload via `vaultAPI.encrypt()`, writes the `.vault` file via the IPC bridge, logs the event to the timeline and awards XP. - Loading a vault prompts for the passphrase, decrypts the file via `vaultAPI.decrypt()`, merges the stored state (tier, XP, role, theme) back into the application and restores the console log. Successful loads log the event and award XP. ### Notes - As external dependency installation is blocked in this environment, AES‑256 support is provided by Node’s native `crypto` module. The encryption routine remains fully offline and consistent across platforms. The revised implementation uses Galois/Counter Mode (GCM) with an authentication tag for integrity checking. ## Added ### Central State (`src/modules/state.js`) - Introduced a persistent state object capturing the current `tier`, accumulated `xp`, current `role`, computed `rank` and selected `theme`. State changes automatically update listeners and persist to `settings.json` in the user data directory via IPC. - Added helper functions to load and save settings, mutate tier, XP, role and theme, and to register change listeners. ### Tier Engine (`src/modules/tierEngine.js`) - Added click handlers to the tier badges in the header. Selecting a numbered tier updates `state.tier`. Selecting the `Founder` or `Sentinel` badges promotes the user to that role and maxes the tier. - Elements tagged with `data-tier-req` are automatically disabled until the required tier is reached. ### XP System (`src/modules/xpSystem.js`) - Added a compact progress bar and rank indicator to the header. The bar fills as the user progresses from Novice (0–9 XP) to Operative (10–49 XP) and Architect (50+ XP). - Added `gainXP()` helper to award XP from other modules. A single message send increments XP by 1 and logs to the timeline, while vault operations award 5 XP. ### Memory Timeline (`src/modules/memoryTimeline.js`) - Implemented a simple logging utility that appends timestamped lines to the timeline panel whenever significant events occur (e.g. send, vault save/load, unlock). Uses `dayjs` if present and falls back to the browser’s locale formatting. ### Vault Encryption (`src/modules/vaultEncryption.js`) - Added encrypted vault save and load functionality using the Web Crypto API (AES‑256‑GCM with a PBKDF2‑derived key). Users are prompted for a passphrase when saving or loading. - Encrypted data is stored to a `.vault` file via the preload API. - Loading merges persisted `tier`, `xp`, `role` and `theme` back into the running state and restores the console log. ### QR/Passphrase Unlock (`src/modules/qrUnlock.js`) - Added a hidden founder unlock panel that accepts either the passphrase `FOUNDER‑KEY‑777` or a file whose name contains “founder” or “unlock”. Successful unlock promotes the user to Founder and awards XP. ### Theme Switcher (`src/modules/themeSwitcher.js`) - Added a theme selector to the header with three built‑in themes: `RetroGreen`, `CyberPurple` and `StealthGray`. Selecting a theme swaps the loaded stylesheet (`themes/*.css`) and persists the choice. - Added the corresponding CSS files under `src/themes/`. ### Self Diagnostic (`src/modules/selfDiagnostic.js`) - Added a diagnostic routine bound to the existing verify button. The routine pings the local LLM endpoint, checks read/write access to the settings directory, counts duplicate `<script>` tags and reports the active theme. Results are printed to the console area as JSON. ### IPC and Main Process Enhancements - Added new IPC handlers in `main.js` for retrieving the user data path, saving and loading JSON settings, and showing a save file dialog. These are exposed to the renderer via `appAPI` in `preload.js`. - Added a graceful import of `electron-updater` and a conditional check for updates when the app is packaged. If the dependency is unavailable, the auto‑update logic is skipped silently. - Added a generic save file dialog for vault export and cleaned up error handling. ### UI Enhancements - Added an XP bar, rank label and theme selector to the header without disturbing the existing layout. - Added a `<link id="themeCSS">` tag for dynamic theme loading. ## Removed - The application no longer relies on third‑party libraries such as `CryptoJS` or `dayjs` at runtime. AES encryption has been reimplemented with the native Web Crypto API and timestamps fall back to the built‑in `Date` API when `dayjs` is unavailable. ## Notes - Because network access is restricted in this environment, it is not possible to fetch NPM dependencies or build the Windows installer locally. The `electron-updater`, `crypto-js`, `dayjs`, `uuid` and `html5-qrcode` dependencies remain declared in `package.json` for completeness but are not strictly required for the application to run. The auto‑update stub will only activate if `electron-updater` is present at runtime. - To fully utilise these features, install the listed dependencies within the `electron_bootstrap` directory (`npm install`) and run the build script (`npm run dist`) on a machine with internet connectivity and the appropriate build tooling.
